I've played numerous Pokemon games over  the years because I enjoyed them for  what they were simple RPGs that offer an  incentive to collect and raise a stable  of quirky creatures but after 20 years  of following the same formula I'm ready  for a change  the pokken tournament the upcoming  fighting game from Bandai Namco Pokemon  battles have evolved giving you total  control over pokemon in a way the series  never has before after almost an hour  with the game's dynamic combat system I  think I found the answer to my Pokemon  problems pokken tournament is a far cry  from the Pokemon games that have defined  the series you don't get to capture and  raise Pokemon in the traditional sense  hookin is a fighting game first and  foremost that shares the same spirit of  Smash Brothers it's an accessible game  with easy-to-understand controls that  allow anyone to jump in and have some  fun but there's also depth for those who  want to hone in and compete on an  advanced level certain moves take  priority over others and while elements  don't play a factor into matchups  between different Pokemon their tendency  is to fight and close their long range  can have an impact on your strategy each  Pokemon has a light and heart attack the  ability to jump block and throw and the  meter based first mode that they can tap  into getting temporary stat buffs and  access to a powerful burst attack until  the meter drains after activation  fights featured two phases which switch  when a pokemon delivers significant  damage to their opponent every match  starts with the field face where two  pokemon move around a 3d space lobbying  long-distance attacks of one another but  once they get close and start to  exchange blows the game typically shifts  into its duel fans where pokemon move on  a fixed 2d plane similar to traditional  fighting games like Street Fighter  during matches you also have the option  to bring out support Pokemon to attack  or interrupt your opponent or lend your  character a brief stat bug once the  Pokemon is called into battle you have  to wait for its gauge to recharge before  you can use it again but it doesn't take  long you typically get to use your  support Pokemon multiple times during a  single fight core mechanics of poking he  fights interesting providing a varied  array of tools that each fighter can use  on top of their already unique movesets  some Pokemon are better up close while  others are optimized for rain of combat  zipping across an arena and dishing a  fantastical attacks feels great and it  gave me a new appreciation for the  creativity and viewed into each poker  without question throwing lightning  bolts as Pikachu is far more exciting  than telling people to do it and just  watching it play yeah the shift in  control felt great and it came easy it  only took a few matches before I had a  handle on any given Pokemon and I could  see how in the hands of a passionate  competitor hook ends true potential as a  fighting game could be unlocked hook and  tournament could be the game that brings  weary players into the series for the  first time and for anyone with an  existing affinity for Nintendo's  creature kitchen classic it may be the  game that refuels their passion in a  suit  changed little since the 90s pokken  tournament is coming out to Wii U in  North America on March 18th
 

